    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d297">

                <pb facs="0366" n="350" xml:id="pg350"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.516 Export-Import Bank/6–1847: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Bolivia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Flack</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">us urgent</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">La Paz</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1947-06-18T12:00:00-04:00">June 18,
                            1947—noon.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>580. During past week pressure from Sucre politicians for change in petroleum
                    program was intensified. Consequently I reiterated to Foreign Minister<anchor
                        n="42" xml:id="d297-f1"/>
                    Exim Bank’s views as furnished in Dept’s
                    313 of May 29. (Refer mytel 553 of June
                        10).<anchor n="43" xml:id="d297-f2"/> In this connection I also had friendly
                    discussion yesterday morning with President Hertzog, who promised that matter
                    would be immediately resolved. Mariaca informed me this morning that at
                    subsequent meeting with Fomento,<anchor n="44" xml:id="d297-f3"/>
                    YPFB officials and Minister of Economy,
                    President stated that original programs approved would be adhered to and ordered
                    that documents in this connection be prepared promptest possible. This appears
                    to settle whole question.</p>
                <p>However, to satisfy Sucre’s aspirations, Bolivian Government may later seek
                    separately to add to its facilities.</p>
                <p>Dept’s 313 has been most useful since it enabled me to continue discussions
                    without requesting additional instructions.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Lima for Sundt.<anchor n="45" xml:id="d297-f4"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Flack</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
